  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nCAMs have been sporadic with precipitation coverage into this\nevening. This leads to lower confidence in exact rain timing,\nbut the general airmass still supports a period of synoptic\nrainfall tonight.\n\nAn area of rain that move through the morning hours has helped\nkeep early afternoon temperatures a few degrees off previous\nforecast highs. This has also likely put a dent in possible\ndestabilization ahead of a warm front lifting into southern New\nEngland. This region of showers did eventually succumb to dry\nair across central Maine, but expect a moistening airmass to\narrive with PWATs near 2 inches.\n\nUpstream convection should make its way towards southern NH this\nevening. Will need to monitor mesoanalysis for any upticks in\nsurface CAPE. This would allow convection to continue to cycle \ninto the southern portion of the forecast area. For now, this\nlooks to stop around the NH/MA border, with elevated \ninstability building up through the ME coast. This much should \nbe useful for increasing rainfall rates through mid evening. The\nabove average PWATs, saturated profiles, and deep warm cloud \nlayers should compliment this with efficient rain processes. Any\ntaller cores that develop could produce rain rates around 1 to \n2 inches per hour. Any training may lead to isolated instances \nof flash flooding, mainly focused south and east of the \nfoothills. However antecedent dry conditions is keeping flash\nflood guidance pretty high.\n\nForcing for the main period of rain is progressive, and\ndiminishing coverage is expected around midnight SW to NE. There\nmay be a few additional showers or rumbles of thunder after\nthis, but PWATs will be diminishing amid less moisture.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA cold front will be approaching western NH come Wednesday\nmorning. This will be bringing in much drier air behind it, but\nsome mid levels will have already dried behind overnight rain.\n\nThis dry air is expected to hinder coverage of showers and\nthunderstorms initiated by the arriving cold front. While CAPE\nbuilds and shear is supportive, think the aspect of severe\nweather remains fairly isolated. It may not be until early\nafternoon that showers or storms develop along the front\nbisecting the CWA. These may contain gusty winds and lightning,\nbut marginal winds in the low levels put a cap on intensity.\nThese showers and storms look to make their way towards the\nME/NH coasts by late afternoon, moving over the coastal waters\ninto the evening.\n\nA cooler night is then in store as dew point temps tumble and\nCAA brings overnight lows in the upper 40s to lower 50s across\nthe area.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe rest of the week and into the weekend look to be more of a \ntranquil setting as high pressure builds into the region. A 500 \nmb broad trough brings in more westerly/northwesterly flow \nkeeping temperatures around normal for this time of year. \nThursday will be the coolest of the days, as the air mass behind\nthe cold front settles into our CWA. The remainder of this week\nlooks to have our area keeping to the low 80s as highs, with \nnighttime temperatures falling into the mid to upper 50s. With \nthese conditions at night, it might be nice to open the windows!\n\nLooking further ahead, the global models are all in decent \nagreement that at the start of next week, a wetter and more \nunsettled pattern will make a return to our area. This is due to\nhigh pressure, which had been dominating, exiting the area. The\nexact weather this far out cannot be set in stone for the time \nbeing, but I think it is fair to say that there looks to be a \ntrend toward slightly cooler (and wetter) weather next week.",
